{
  "name": "Corporation: founders buy restricted common stock with a one-year cliff and monthly vesting",
  "description": "A Delaware corporation authorizes 10,000,000 common shares in its charter. Two founders each sign a restricted stock purchase agreement: 4,800,000 and 2,400,000 shares at $0.001 a share, par $0.0001, so par goes to share capital (3100) and the rest to paid-in capital (3110). Each founder is their own party and their own contract, from one template with the shares as terms, and every contract binds the charter's share ledger (read from the charter contract's state) so they draw on one authorization. Shares are outstanding and unvested from the purchase; 25% vests at the one-year cliff and 1/48 monthly after it, each a scheduled activity whose service evidence is elapsed time, so the ledger's timer records it on its date (without it every tranche plans as contingent and never vests). Tax elections (83(b)) and options are not modeled.",
